Thanks to David Knight (David.Knight@xxxxxxxxx.xxx) and Martin R=F8nde=20
Andersen (martin.roende@xxxxxxxx.xxx) along with one other person whose=20
email I cannot find.

David suggested taking a look at disk writes across cluster nodes (I=20
neglected to mention we are clustered). We have not done this as yet but=20
will definitely do so. David also supplied a perl script that allows=20
read/write stats for disks included in a clustered volume, and I've long=20
suspected that some of our problems lie here.

Martin noted that we may well be saturating the disks no matter which node=
 they're on, so moving a disk to the other node may not prove useful.

We also opened a ticket with HP, and after looking at our collect output,=
 the engineer recommended changing an /etc/sysconfigtab parameter=20
(ssm_threshhold) from 0 to 8388608. The collective memory indicates that=20
this parameter was set to 0 at Oracle's suggestion. Anyway, once we=20
changed the parameter (no reboot required), we were able to unzip and ls=20
simultaneously without one process blocking the other. We are not seeing=20
improved performance (adding weight to Martin's contention) but the=20
presenting problem has been resolved.
=20
